    {
      "id": "reason-bayes",
      "answer": "**≈ 1 % (about 1 in 100)**  \n\nWith a prevalence of 1 / 10 000 (0.01 %) and a test that is 99 % sensitive and 99 % specific, a positive result yields:\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{disease}\\mid +) = \\frac{0.99 \\times 0.0001}{0.99 \\times 0.0001 + 0.01 \\times 0.9999} \\approx 0.0098\n\\]\n\nThe disease is so rare that false positives (1 % of 9 999 healthy people ≈ 100) vastly outnumber true positives (99 % of 1 sick person ≈ 1), so a positive test only raises the probability from 0.01 % to roughly 1 %.",
      "frames": 208,
      "latencyMs": 69724,
      "costCents": null,
      "textSource": null,
      "error": null
    },
    {
      "id": "reason-tradeoff",
      "answer": "**No.** At ~30 requests/minute a single, properly configured PostgreSQL instance will handle the load easily. A read replica adds an extra network hop (and potential replication lag), increases cost, and introduces operational overhead (fail-over, monitoring, schema changes) that a two-person team doesn’t need at this scale.\n\nReconsider only if read traffic grows by orders of magnitude or you require built-in high-availability with automatic fail-over.\n\n## Sources\n- [Source](https://servodatabase.com/servo/mpi/mx-30)",
      "frames": 99,
      "latencyMs": 33750,
      "costCents": null,
      "textSource": null,
      "error": null
    },
